classical.excerpt:
لا قيد على الحد~$t$ في~\RightR{\lexists}
و~\LeftR{\lforall} من جهة المتغير المميَّز. وأما~\LeftR{\lexists}
و~\RightR{\lforall} فشرطهما ألا يقع ال!!{constant}~$a$ في التتابعية العليا خارج~$!A(a)$.
ولا تستقيم سلامة النظام من غير هذا الشرط، أعني ألا !!{derive}s إلا التتابعيات الصالحة. إذ لو أُسقط لجاز ما يأتي:

english.excerpt:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every !!{derivable}~$!A$ is \iftag{FOL}{valid}{a tautology};
\item if !!a{sentence} is !!{derivable} from some others, it is also a
  consequence of them;
\item if a set of !!{sentence}s is inconsistent, it is unsatisfiable.
\end{enumerate}
These are important properties of !!a{derivation} system.  If any of
them do not hold, the !!{derivation} system is deficient---it would
!!{derive} too much.  Consequently, establishing the soundness of a
!!{derivation} system is of the utmost importance.

supporting_primary_witnesses.excerpt:
\begin{defn}
  \ollabel{defn:enumerable}
  A set~$A$ is !!{enumerable} iff either $A = \emptyset$ or there is
  an enumeration of~$A$. We say that $A$ is !!{nonenumerable} iff $A$
  is not !!{enumerable}.
\end{defn}

In \LeftR{\lforall}, $t$ is a closed term (i.e., one without
variables). In \RightR{\lforall}, $a$~is !!a{constant} which must not
occur anywhere in the lower sequent of the \RightR{\lforall} rule. We
call $a$ the \emph{eigenvariable} of the \RightR{\forall}
inference.\footnote{We use the term ``eigenvariable'' even though $a$
in the above rule is !!a{constant}. This has historical reasons.}
